      "type": "summary",
      "text": "## Executive summary\n\n**The late-delivery rate is driven by Shipping Mode: First Class runs at 95.3% against 54.8% overall (+40.5 pp).**\n\n**Key drivers**\n- **Shipping Mode:** First Class at 95.3% vs 54.8% overall (+40.5 pp). From LATAM (94.7%) to Africa (97.0%): the problem holds in every Market.\n\n**Recommendations**\n1. **Reset the promised lead time for First Class to what the network actually delivers.** Impact: High, effort: Low.\n2. **Audit promised versus actual lead times across every shipping mode.** Impact: High, effort: Medium.\n3. **Run an execution review of First Class with carriers and warehouses.** Impact: High, effort: High.\n\n## Question\n\nWhat is driving late deliveries, and what should we do about it? Measured by the late-delivery rate: Share of order lines delivered late (0 to 1). Cancelled lines count as not late.\n\n## Issue tree and findings\n\n| Branch | Hypothesis | Verdict | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Logistics: Shipping Mode | Some shipping modes miss their promised lead time far more often. | **Driver** | First Class at 95.3% vs 54.8% overall (+40.5 pp). |\n| Geography: Market | Deliveries are late in particular regions. | Ruled out | Every reliable segment is within 5 pp of 54.8%; worst is Europe at 55.2% (+0.4 pp). |\n| Customer: Customer Segment | Some customer types get worse service. | Ruled out | Every reliable segment is within 5 pp of 54.8%; worst is Home Office at 55.1% (+0.2 pp). |\n| Product: Category | Certain products are harder to deliver on time. | Ruled out | Every reliable segment is within 5 pp of 54.8%; worst is Accessories at 57.0% (+2.1 pp). |\n| Time: quarterly trend | Lateness has got worse recently. | Ruled out | Every reliable period is within 5 pp of 54.8%; worst is 2015Q3 at 55.9% (+1.1 pp). |\n\n## Impact\n\n- **First Class:** 26,513 of 27,814 order lines were late, carrying $5.41M in sales (14.7% of company sales) and $617K in profit.\n- By total sales, Standard Class contributes the most ($22.02M, 59.9% of the total) and Same Day the least ($1.94M, 5.3%).\n- These are sales and profit exposed to late delivery, not measured losses.\n\n## Recommendations\n\n| # | Action | Why | Impact | Effort | First step |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Reset the promised lead time for First Class to what the network actually delivers | Shipping Mode: First Class at 95.3% vs 54.8% overall (+40.5 pp). | High | Low | Compare promised and actual shipping days for First Class, then update the customer promise. |\n| 2 | Audit promised versus actual lead times across every shipping mode | A company-wide safeguard alongside the fixes above. | High | Medium | Pull promised and actual shipping days per order for one month. |\n| 3 | Run an execution review of First Class with carriers and warehouses | Shipping Mode: First Class at 95.3% vs 54.8% overall (+40.5 pp). | High | High | Agree an on-time target for First Class with each carrier and track it weekly. |\n\n## Method and caveats\n\n- Rule: a branch is a driver if its worst segment with at least 1,000 order lines is more than 5 pp worse than the baseline. Smaller segments are too noisy to act on.\n- Counts are order lines (a product within an order), not whole orders.\n- Periods with unusually low volume were left out of the trend test: 2017Q4, 2018Q1.\n- Produced by the scripted brain: fixed rules, no AI model. Every number comes from a tool result."
